          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of manifold-diagrammatic higher categories (in some
            %a{:href => "#geocats"} places
            also referred to as a ‘geometric’ or ‘geometrical’ higher categories) broadly refers to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher categories
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operation"} operation
            is modelled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified-geometric
            notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+diagram"} manifold diagrams
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            Models of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher categories
            are usually defined as certain collections of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphisms"} k-morphisms
            that are parametrized by (that is,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheafs
            on) some category of ‘combinatorial shapes’: examples include presheafs of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+set"} globular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetope"} opetopic
            shapes.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+diagram"} Manifold diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss"} trusses
            (or, more precisely,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trusses#comb_mdiag"} combinatorial manifold diagrams
          %h3#embeddings_of_manifold_diagrams Embeddings of manifold diagrams
          %h4#geometric_idea Geometric idea
          %p
            %em Embeddings
            of manifold
            = succeed "-diagrams" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+diagram#framed_map_euclidean_case"} framed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified maps
            whose underlying maps are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} embeddings
            of spaces (this is different from the notion of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space#stratified_map_types"} substratifications
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/mdiag_inclusions_and_coarsenings_v2.svg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 600px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption
                Figure 1: Embeddings of manifold diagrams (for which
                %i substratifications
                and
                %i coarsenings
                form a factorization system).
          %p
            Geometric dualization unveils that embeddings of manifold diagrams are precisely ‘cellular’ maps of their dual framed cell complexes (‘cellular’ is usually meant to imply that maps map closed cells to closed cells), which can be seen to have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28epi%2Cmono%29+factorization+system"} (epi,mono) factorization system
            by degeneracy and face maps. The next figure illustrates this: the shown maps are precisely the the geometric duals of the maps in the previous figure.
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/mdiag_faces_and_degeneracies_v4.svg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 600px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption
                Figure 2: Geometric duals of embeddings are cellular maps (for which
                %i face
                and
                %i degeneracy
                maps form a factorization system)
          %h4#combinatorial_definition Combinatorial definition
          %p Combinatorially, we define the following.
