          %h3#quotients_of_manifold_diagrams Quotients of manifold diagrams
          %h4#geometric_idea_2 Geometric idea
          %p
            %em Quotients
            of manifold diagrams are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+diagram#framed_map_euclidean_case"} framed
            stratified maps whose underlying maps are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient maps
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/mdiag_quotients_v2.svg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 600px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption
                Figure 3: Quotients of manifold diagrams are the
                %i
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/n-truss#duality"} categorically dual notion
                to embeddings.
          %p Geometric dualization unveils that quotients of manifold diagrams correspond to subdivisions of framed cell complexes. The next figure illustrates the geometric duals of the quotient maps in the preceding figure.
          %center
            %figure
              %img{:alt => "", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/mdiag_subdivisions_v2.svg", :style => "width:90%; max-width: 600px; height:auto;"}/
              %figcaption Figure 4: The geometric duals of quotients are subdivisions.
          %h4#combinatorial_definition_2 Combinatorial definition
          %p Combinatorially, we define the following.
          %p
          #def_quotients.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %em Quotients of combinatorial manifold diagrams
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T, S
              are maps of labeled trusses
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ↠
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T \twoheadrightarrow S
            %p
              whose underlying map of trusses is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#n-truss_bundle_map"} singular
              and surjective.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              To summarize: while the notion embeddings of manifold diagrams recovers (after geometric dualization) face and degeneracy maps often found in the combinatorics of shapes, in contrast, the notion of quotients dual to that of embeddings captures (after geometric dualization) something new: namely, subdivisions of cells. This is quite powerful e.g. for describing compositions as we will exploit below.(Moreover, it turns out that classifying subdivisions is generally a difficult (undecidable) problem, see e.g.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#DornDouglas21"} Dorn-Douglas 2021
          %h3#sketch_definition Sketch Definition
          %p
            We first organize embeddings and quotients (in the sense of Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#def_embeddings"}
            resp. Def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#def_quotients"}
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Terminology
            %p
              Together, embeddings and quotients organize into the
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕄
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi Diag
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{M}\mathsf{Diag}_n
                  of combinatorial manifold
                  = succeed "-diagrams" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
