            %p
              (note that the dashed arrow is unique if it exists.) The
              %em
                category of manifold
                = succeed "-diagrams" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi MDiag
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{MDiag}_n
              will refer to just the horizontal part of this double category.
          %p
            When defining higher categories parametrized by manifold
            = succeed "-diagrams," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p#SketchDefinition
            %strong>
              %em Sketch definition
            \. A
            %em
              manifold diagrammatic
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            should be a presheaf on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi MDiag
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{MDiag}_n
            subject to the following two conditions.
          %ol
            %li
              The collection of morphisms parametrized by a given diagram should be determined by subdiagrams that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
              it. Formally, this can be enforced by an appropriate
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              condition.
            %li
              The composition of a given diagram should be compatible with the composition of its subdiagrams. Formally, this can be enforced by requiring that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              extends to a double-(co)presheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕄
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Diag
                      %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                        %mo ,
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} co
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi et
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{M}\mathsf{Diag}^{\mathrm{op},\mathrm{co}}_n \to \mathbb{S}\mathbf{et}
              (covariant in the vertical part, where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi et
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}\mathbf{et}
              is the double category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category#examples"} squares
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Sq
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Sq}(\mathbf{Set})
              in
              = succeed "))." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              (Coherence conditions). What’s worth pointing out about the above definition sketch is that there are no explicit higher-categorical coherence conditions (like
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex#KanComplexes"} filler
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+condition"} Segal
              conditions) beyond ordinary sheaf conditions. This is because coherences can be expressed internally to the category of manifold diagrams (namely, as so-called ‘manifold-diagram
              = succeed "\u2019" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopies"} isotopies
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
          %p#mdiag_infty
          #mdiag_infty.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              (The
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
              case). So far we worked with
              = succeed "-diagrams" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              which work well as parametrizing objects for
              = succeed "-categories." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
              as follows. Given a combinatorial manifold
              = succeed "-diagram" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#cylinder"} cylinder
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mo ×
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I} \times T
              is a combinatorial manifold
              = succeed "-diagram." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi MDiag
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{MDiag}
              (resp. the double category
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕄
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Diag
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{M}\mathsf{Diag}
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,\infty)
          %h3#a_formal_definition A formal definition
          %p To make the preceding sketch precise, one would need to formalize the sheaf condition. We now mention one way of how this could be achieved.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Terminology
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}_n
              (and similarly,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}
              from Rmk.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#mdiag_infty"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategory"} wide
              subcategories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^L_n
              resp.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^R_n
              consisting of spans
