          %p
            We define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^R_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                    %mi n
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^R_n
            does not have all pullbacks since, e.g., subdiagrams can intersect in non-diagrams.)
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em neighborhood coverage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi J
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_n
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^R_n
              is the coverage that assigns to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \in \mathrm{MDiag}
              the single family
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f_x : T^{\leq x} \to T \}_{x \in T}
              comprised of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#stratified_neighborhood"} stratified neighborhoods
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p We may now formalize our earlier sketch definition as follows.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                manifold-diagrammatic
                = succeed "-category" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a presheaf on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}_n
              such that:
            %ol
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                        %mi n
                        %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi J
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathrm{MDiag}^R_n,J_n)
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+sheaf"} locally constant
                on (i.e. constant on the connected components of)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} MDiag
                          %mi n
                          %mi L
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{MDiag}^L_n
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                extends to a double-(co)presheaf
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕄
                        %msubsup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi Diag
                          %mi n
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                            %mo ,
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} co
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝕊
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi et
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{M}\mathsf{Diag}^{\mathrm{op},\mathrm{co}}_n \to \mathbb{S}\mathbf{et}
            %p
          %p (Note, however, that the practicality of this definition has not yet been established, and several variants are under consideration, cf. next section.)
          %h3#variants Variants
          %p Instead of considering presheafs on combinatorial manifold diagrams, one may also consider
          %ul
            %li
              presheafs on (unlabeled)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss"} trusses
            %li
              presheafs on (unlabeled)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-truss#%23atomic_trusses_terminology"} atomic trusses
          %p with various sheaf conditions in place. In both cases, faces, degeneracies and compositions may be understood via mappings as outlined above. The potential advantage of dropping labelings from our parametrizing objects in this way (i.e. considering trusses in place of labeled trusses) is simplicity. For instance, an immediate simplification is that we can work with truss maps instead of spans of truss maps when combinatorially modelling embeddings. This could simplify the comparison to other presheaf models of higher categories.
          %p
            Another related, potentially useful observation is that the spans occurring in the definition of the category of manifold diagrams may be expressed concisely in terms of bundles (slightly generalizing the notion of labeled
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trusses"} truss bordism
          %p
            Several other approaches to and variations of the idea of manifold-diagrammatic higher categories are currently under consideration. The categories of combinatorial shapes considered here (whether combinatorial manifold diagrams, or (atomic) trusses, or similar) often have peculiarities that one doesn’t encounter in other settings (e.g. failure to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
            due to the novel concept of reduction). The precise nature of these shape categories remains to be fully understood.
